\description{
Estimates the density of a 3D point cloud, and draws surfaces of constant
density enclosing given fractions of it: the 3D analog of
\code{\link[ggplot2:geom_density_2d]{ggplot2::geom_density_2d()}}. Each surface bounds the smallest region
containing a given fraction of the estimated probability mass (a "highest
density region"), so that e.g. \code{probs = 0.5} shows where the densest half of
the data lie.
}
\details{
The density is a Gaussian kernel density estimate, evaluated on a grid that
extends beyond the data far enough for surfaces to close, and surfaces are
triangulated with the marching cubes algorithm. Where limits are set on the
plot's scales, e.g. with \code{\link[ggplot2:xlim]{ggplot2::xlim()}} or \code{\link[=zlim]{zlim()}}, points outside them
are removed before the density is estimated, as elsewhere in ggplot2, and
surfaces are cut at them, leaving them open there unless \code{cap = TRUE}. Cutting
doesn't change the fractions of mass the surfaces enclose, which refer to the
whole estimate.
}
\section{Aesthetics}{

\code{stat_isodensity_3d()} requires the following aesthetics:
\itemize{
\item \strong{x}, \strong{y}, \strong{z}: Point coordinates
}

and understands \strong{weight}, giving points' relative weights in the
density. Grouping aesthetics give a separate density for each group.

\code{geom_isodensity_3d()} draws surfaces like \code{\link[=geom_isosurface_3d]{geom_isosurface_3d()}}, and
understands the same aesthetics.
}

\section{Computed variables}{

\itemize{
\item \code{prob}: The fraction of probability mass the surface encloses
\item \code{level}: The density on the surface
\item \code{normal_x}, \code{normal_y}, \code{normal_z}: Components of each triangle's unit
normal, pointing outward from the region the surface encloses
\item \code{cap}: Whether a triangle is part of a cap (see \code{cap})
}
}

\section{Dependencies}{

Surfaces are computed with the \pkg{rmarchingcubes} package, which must be
installed.
